# From Lookup Choice to Governed Write

A lookup result is a candidate, not write authorization. This workbook carries a
selected value into an Updato update and makes the host revalidate current
membership, tenant scope, and business status before SQL can be compiled.

## Setup

```elixir
Code.require_file(Path.expand("support/bootstrap.exs", __DIR__))
SelectoLivebooksNotebookBootstrap.install_verification!()
```

## 1) Declare the choice source on the writable field

The choice source names a governed lookup contract. The host remains responsible
for resolving it; Selecto does not open another domain or trust a browser label.

```elixir
import ExUnit.Assertions

domain = %{
  source: %{
    source_table: "work_items", primary_key: :id,
    fields: [:id, :assignee_id],
    columns: %{
      id: %{type: :integer},
      assignee_id: %{type: :integer, choice_source: :assignees}
    },
    associations: %{}
  },
  schemas: %{},
  choice_sources: %{
    assignees: %{domain: :people, value_field: :id, label_field: :name}
  },
  writes: %{
    operations: %{update: %{enabled: true}},
    fields: %{assignee_id: %{updatable: true}}
  }
}

assert :ok = SelectoUpdato.validate_domain(domain)
```

## 2) Treat the lookup response as untrusted request data

This synthetic response has the same boundary as a co-domain result returned to
a client. Only the value is submitted. Its label is presentation data and is not
copied into the mutation.

```elixir
lookup_result = %{value: "7", label: "Ada — active"}
submitted_value = String.to_integer(lookup_result.value)
assert submitted_value == 7
```

## 3) Revalidate membership while compiling the write

The resolver receives the typed value, equality-filter record context, trusted
tenant, and host-supplied choice filters. A real host can rerun the co-domain's
active/member predicate here or call an equivalent policy service.

```elixir
test_pid = self()
active_members = MapSet.new([{7, 7}])

resolver = fn %Selecto.Domain.Choices.Request{} = request ->
  send(test_pid, {:membership_check, request})

  if MapSet.member?(active_members, {request.tenant, request.value}) and
       request.filters == [{:tenant_id, 7}] do
    Selecto.Domain.Choices.valid(:active_tenant_member)
  else
    Selecto.Domain.Choices.invalid(:outside_current_choice_set)
  end
end

operation =
  domain
  |> SelectoUpdato.new(
    tenant: %{tenant_id: 7},
    choice_source_filters: [{:tenant_id, 7}],
    choice_source_membership_resolver: resolver
  )
  |> SelectoUpdato.filter({:id, 42})
  |> SelectoUpdato.update(%{assignee_id: submitted_value})

assert {:ok, %Selecto.Write.Command{} = command, _context} =
  SelectoUpdato.PortableCommand.compile(operation)
assert_receive {:membership_check, request}
assert request.value == 7
assert request.record == %{id: 42, assignee_id: 7}
assert request.tenant == 7
assert request.metadata == %{surface: :updato, operation: :update}
command
```

## 4) Fail closed when the proof is absent or stale

Removing the resolver is an error, not an implicit allow. A value that was once
visible but is now inactive or belongs to another tenant is rejected too.

```elixir
without_resolver =
  domain
  |> SelectoUpdato.new()
  |> SelectoUpdato.filter({:id, 42})
  |> SelectoUpdato.update(%{assignee_id: 7})

assert {:error, %SelectoUpdato.Error{
  type: :validation,
  details: %{code: :choice_membership_resolver_required, fields: ["assignee_id"]}
}} = SelectoUpdato.PortableCommand.compile(without_resolver)

outside_choice_set = %{operation | changes: %{assignee_id: 8}}
assert {:error, %SelectoUpdato.Error{type: :validation}} =
  SelectoUpdato.PortableCommand.compile(outside_choice_set)
assert_receive {:membership_check, %{value: 8}}
```

The same rule applies to action inputs: display choices may be cached, but
membership must be checked against trusted current context at preview/apply time.
See [governed lookups](selecto_co_domains_workbook.livemd) for the read side.
